st count across those loads. Bootstrap selection uses existing applicable constraints: default unified-v2 inputs, the npm 230 ms ceiling, retained floors, successful outcomes and clean final memory. No PyPI ceiling existed during sample selection. The new PyPI ceiling is derived after selection and governs future observations. Recorded maxima can exceed that ceiling because the formula uses the lowest per-shape maximum. The original npm maxima and 230 ms ceiling keep their historical source group and are not recomputed from these new observations. The new PyPI maxima use only the final three qualifying runs. Each ecosystem uses the same ceiling formula independently because its floors depend on its own injected RTT. Runs 38502988119 and 38513015540 are wholly excluded because a profile exceeded the existing npm ceiling. They contribute no counts or latency maxima. Failures and adverse observations remain in the corresponding workflow reports. Future runs above either applicable native ceiling are unheld as a whole. The final 3 run records, 40016327682, 40018954941, 40019366641, measured unified-v2 at e72c13663bbccc4cd2f49b2d2e418f0a5bd284b0. Each supplies one qualifying observation under each of the four scheduled pod shapes. They add only the npm/slow-readers and pypi/slow-readers loaded floors: half the lowest count of either load, rounded down, and at least 1. Four runs dispatched at the same commit are wholly excluded, because a profile exceeded the PyPI ceiling of 20 ms. Run 39958149768 injected 22 ms under 4cpu-2gib and 23 ms under unlimited. Run 39985720638 injected 32 ms under 4cpu-2gib. Run 39991179217 injected 70 ms under 2cpu-1gib. Run 40020024081 injected 29 to 32 ms under every shape. They contribute no counts or latency maxima. Runs 40022928943, 40023792987, 40025832132 and 40027186864, dispatched at the same commit, were cancelled once three runs qualified, and contribute nothing. These runs change no earlier floor and no latency record. The next 12 r